solving this equation algebraically is too complex. Therefore, perhaps numerical methods or graphing could help, but the problem asks for the sum of all real roots, suggesting it can be solved using properties of equations or substitutions.\n\nWait, alternatively, use substitution u = x -1/x. Wait, in problems involving x + 1/x, substitution u =x +1/x. Here, we have x + something.\n\nAlternatively, note that if x is a root, then perhaps 1/x is also involved. Let’s consider the reciprocal substitution.\n\nLet’s suppose that y =1/x. Then, substitute y, but x is involved in the equation. Original equation:\n\nx + x / sqrt(x2 -1) =35/12.\n\nIf I let y=1/x, then x=1/y. But we need to express everything in terms of y. Let\'s try:\n\nLeft-hand side: x + x /sqrt(x2 -1) = 1/y + (1/y)/sqrt( (1/y)^2 -1 )\n\nSimplify denominator sqrt(1/y2 -1) = sqrt( (1 - y2)/y2 )= sqrt(1 - y2)/|y|. Since x is real, and x>1 or x<-1, so y=1/x would be in (0,1) if x>1 or y<-1. Assuming real roots, y is either less than -1 or between 0 and1, but since x is positive when y is negative reciprocal. Hmm, complicated.\n\nSo let\'s let y =1/x, then equation becomes:\n\n1/y + (1/y) / sqrt( (1/y)^2 -1 ) =35/12\n\nLet’s simplify the denominator:\n\nsqrt( (1/y2) -1 )=sqrt( (1 - y2)/y2 )= sqrt(1 - y2)/|y|\n\nThus, if y>0, denominator is sqrt(1 - y2)/y. If y<0, denominator is sqrt(1 - y2)/|y|. Since y=1/x and x>1 or x<-1, so y is either in (0,1) or (-1,0). So |y|=1/y when y>0 and |y|= -y when y<0.\n\nTherefore, let’s consider two cases:\n\nCase 1: y>0 (x>1)\n\nsqrt(x2 -1)=sqrt(1/y2 -1)=sqrt((1 - y2)/y2)=sqrt(1 - y2)/y\n\nTherefore, term (1/y)/sqrt( (1/y2)-1 )= (1/y) / (sqrt(1 - y2)/y )=1 / sqrt(1 - y2)\n\nSo the equation becomes:\n\n1/y +1/sqrt(1 - y2)=35/12\n\nBut x is in (1, ∞), y in (0,1). So equation:\n\n1/y +1/sqrt(1 - y2)=35/12\n\nCase 2: y<0 (x<-1)\n\nSimilarly, sqrt(x2 -1)=sqrt(1/y2 -1)=sqrt((1 - y2)/y2)=sqrt(1 - y2)/|y|=sqrt(1 - y2)/(-y) since y<0.\n\nTherefore, term (1/y)/sqrt(1 - y2)/(-y)= -1/sqrt(1 - y2)\n\nSo equation becomes:\n\n1/y -1/sqrt(1 - y2)=35/12\n\nSince y<0, x < -1.\n\nTherefore, we have two equations to consider depending on the sign of y.\n\nCase 1: For y>0,\n\n1/y +1/sqrt(1 - y2)=35/12.\n\nLet’s solve for y.\n\nLet’s set t = y, so equation is:\n\n1/t +1/sqrt(1 - t2)=35/12, t ∈(0,1).\n\nLet’s find t>0 satisfying this.\n\nLet me define function f(t)=1/t +1/sqrt(1 -t2) and solve f(t)=35/12.\n\nIt’s challenging to solve analytically. Let’s check possible t.\n\nLet’s test t=0.1:\n\n1/0.1=10, sqrt(1 -0.01)=sqrt(0.99)≈0.995. 1/0.995≈1.005. So 10 +1.005≈11.005. 11.005 >35/12≈2.916. Way too big.\n\nt=0.5:\n\n1/0.5=2, sqrt(1 -0.25)=sqrt(0.75)=~0.866. 1/0.866≈1.154. Sum=2 +1.154≈3.154 >35/12. Still too big.\n\nt=0.4:\n\n1/0.4=2.5, sqrt(1 -0.16)=sqrt(0.84)=≈0.917, 1/0.917≈1.093. Sum≈2.5 +1.093≈3.593>35/12.\n\nt=0.3:\n\n1/0.3≈3.333, sqrt(1 -0.09)=sqrt(0.91)=≈0.953, 1/0.953≈1.048. Sum≈4.381>35/12.\n\nt=0.2:\n\n5, sqrt(0.96)=≈0.98, 1/0.98≈1.02. Sum≈6.02 still too big.\n\nt approaching 0, f(t) approaches infinity. At t=1-, sqrt(1-t2)=0, f(t)=1/t +infty, which would approach infinity.\n\nWait, but as t approaches 1 from below, 1/t approaches 1, and 1/sqrt(1-t2) approaches +infty. So function f(t) tends to infinity.\n\nSince at t=0.1, f(t)=~11.005 and it decreases to ~3.154 at t=0.5, then as t increases from 0 to1, f(t) decreases from ~infinity to 3.154.\n\nWe need f(t)=35/12≈2.916. Since at t=0.5, f(t)=3.154, which is more than 35/12, and as t approaches 1, f(t) tends to 1 + infty= infty, but how? Wait, no. At t approaching 1 from below, 1/t approaches1, and 1/sqrt(1-t2) approaches infty. Therefore, f(t) tends to infty. Wait, but in previous calculation at t=0.9: let\'s check.\n\nt=0.9:\n\n1/0.9≈1.111, sqrt(1 -0.81)=sqrt(0.19)=≈0.435, 1/0.435≈2.294. Sum≈1.111+2.294≈3.405, which is still more than 2.916.\n\nWait, maybe miscalculations. Wait, t=0.9:\n\n1/t=1.111, 1/sqrt(1 -0.81)=1/sqrt(0.19)=≈2.294. Sum≈3.405>35/12.\n\nWait, but if t=0.8:\n\n1/0.8=1.25, sqrt(1 -